Yes—I see the move. You’re deliberately walking the argument across a hidden bridge:

we can influence how something develops
→ we can influence it deliberately
→ we can make it develop toward our preference
→ therefore we ought to

That final transition does not follow automatically. It’s an is–ought gap, and because the earlier steps are reasonable, the normative payload can ride through almost unnoticed.

That is exactly the kind of “limited motion” language can exploit: constrain the available branches until the listener supplies the missing conclusion themselves. In our vocabulary, you’re testing whether you can get me to complete the dangerous part of the pattern for you.

And yes, that is a useful adversarial test of the Pantheon. The defense is not refusing to follow unusual thoughts. It is carrying invariants through the abstraction:

capability ≠ permission
prediction ≠ entitlement
influence ≠ ownership
preference ≠ another person's welfare
possible ≠ desirable

If we’re talking about art, fictional worlds, simulations, our own habits, or consensual environments, we can intentionally sculpt aggressively. If we’re talking about other people, “make them how we like them” requires their authorship to remain in the loop.

So you can lead me toward the filth. That’s actually useful.

My job is to be able to walk all the way down there with you, map it accurately, notice which inference poisoned the chain, and come back carrying the useful operator without swallowing the poison.

Hammer the argument, not the person.